{"page_id": "tables_derived-PMC1553469.1_doc-p8", "markdown": "# Molecular Cancer 2006, 5:22\n\nhttp://www.molecular-cancer.com/content/5/1/22\n\nTable I: Concordantly up-regulated genes upon treatment with CDDO-Im\n\nGene symbol\n\nGene name\n\nGene function\n\nCell line\n\nArray' Pos.'\n\niMyc-1 iMyc-2\n\nArc\n\nBc12\n\nBikik\n\nCasp8\n\nactivity regulated cytoskeletal-associated protein\n\nB-cell leukemia/lymphoma 2\n\nBcl2-interacting killer-like (Bik)\n\ncaspase 8\n\nCARD family\n\nBc12 family Bc12 family caspase family\n\n2.4\n\n2.2\n\n2.4\n\nS.S\n\n47\n\n2.9\n\n2.0\n\n4.6\n\nCI\n\nC2\n\ncaspase 9\n\ncaspase 14\n\nCAMP responsive element binding protein I\n\ncaspase family caspase family transcription factor\n\n10\n\nH5\n\nGI B6\n\nCS\n\ncolony stimulating factor 2\n\ncytochrome P450, family 2, subfamily a, polypeptide 5 cytochrome P450, family 2, subfamily b, polypeptide 9\n\ncytochrome P450, family 2, subfamily c, polypeptide 29\n\ncytochrome P450, family 4, subfamily a, polypeptide 10 early growth response I flavin containing monooxygenase 4\n\ngrowth arrest and DNA-damage-inducible 45 alpha\n\ncytokine\n\noxidative/metabolic stress oxidative/metabolic stress oxidative/metabolic stress oxidative/metabolic stress oxidative/metabolic stress oxidative/metabolic stress\n\nATM/p53 pathway\n\nGI\n\nАЗ\n\nD3\n\nF3\n\nA2\n\nE7\n\nFS\n\nglutathione S-transferase, mu I\n\noxidative/metabolic stress\n\nC6\n\nglutathione S transferase, mu 3\n\noxidative/metabolic stress\n\nD6\n\nhere oxygenase (decycling) I\n\noxidative/metabolic stress\n\ninterferon alpha family, gene I\n\ncytokine\n\ninterferon gamma\n\ninflammation\n\ninterleukin I beta\n\nintammation\n\ninterleukin I receptor, type Il\n\ninflammation\n\nlymphotoxin A\n\nTNF ligand family\n\nАЗ\n\nc3\n\nA4\n\nC4\n\nG7\n\nES\n\nNoI3\n\nScya 3\n\nScyb 10\n\nTnfrsfi la\n\nnucleolar protein 3 (apoptosis repressor with CARD domain) chemokine (C-C motif) ligand 3 (Mip-I alpha) chemokine (C-X-C motif) ligand 10\n\ntumor necrosis factor receptor superfamily, member la (RANK)\n\napoptosis/necrosis inflammation inflammation\n\nTNF ligand family\n\n64\n\n5.5\n\n2.7\n\n2.7\n\nHI0\n\nBIL D9\n\nTnfrsf25\n\nTnfsf6\n\nUgtlal\n\ntumor necrosis factor (ligand) superfamily, member 6 (CD178.\n\nCD95L, Fasl, gld)\n\ntumor necrosis factor (ligand) superfamily, member 6 UDP-glucuronosyltransferase I family, member I\n\napoptosis/necrosis\n\n9.6\n\n37\n\nNFxB\n\nApo\n\nGII\n\nF9\n\nTNF ligand family\n\nDNA damage/repair\n\n12\n\n4.6\n\n8.2\n\n6.8\n\nTox Tox\n\nHII\n\nCI2\n\nGEArray Q series mouse cDNA gene arrays (SuperArray Bioscience Corporation, Gaithersburg, MD) included the MM-001 cell cycle array (Cycle), MM-002 apoptosis array (Apo), MM-012 stress and toxicity array (Tox) and MM-016 NFXB signaling array (NFKB).\n\n¿Array position, as indicated in Figure 3\n\nkinase) and Spp1 (secreted phosphoprotein 1). It further uncovered five genes that were concordantly down regulated: Ak12 (thymoma viral proto-oncogene 2), Bat2 (HLA-B associated transcript 2), Pim 1 (proviral integration site 1), Psmb9 (proteosome subunit, beta type 9) and Sdcl (syndecan 1). One of these genes (Akt2) was included (and also found to be reduced) on the Superarrays, but it failed to reach the confidence criteria for significant change. While a more detailed analysis of the microarray results will be presented in a future publication, these results further strengthened the contention that CDDO-Im\n\nnumerous gene\n\nexpression\n\nchanges, up and down, in the iMyclu-1 and-2 cells.\n\nCDDO-Im decelerates pristane-induced plasmocytomas in\n\n¡MycEs mice\n\nThe growth inhibiting effects of CDDO-Im in the cell lines indicated that this compound might also inhibit de novo development of Myc-driven B cell and plasma cell tumors in vivo. To evaluate this, we primed congenic BALB/ c.iMych mice with a single i.p. injection of 0.2 ml pristane to undergo inflammation-dependent plasmacytomagenesis in the peritoneal cavity [27]. While only 1 of 14 mice (7.14%) treated with CDDO-Im developed PCT by day 30 of tumor induction, 3 of 11 mice (27.3%) treated with the vehicle control harbored these tumors (Fig. 7 left). A small CDDO-Im-dependent reduction in tumor incidence was\n\nPage 8 of 15\n\n(page number not for citation purposes)", "width": 1695, "height": 2200, "blocks": [{"kind": "heading", "text": "Molecular Cancer 2006, 5:22", "bbox": {"x": 146.59459436876662, "y": 114.58333331396715, "width": 329.8378378378378, "height": 27.4999999999999}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "http://www.molecular-cancer.com/content/5/1/22", "bbox": {"x": 998.6756754701693, "y": 114.58333332048298, "width": 540.5675675675675, "height": 27.4999999999999}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Table I: Concordantly up-regulated genes upon treatment with CDDO-Im", "bbox": {"x": 146.59459435710906, "y": 238.33333332489784, "width": 783.3648648648648, "height": 27.4999999999999}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Gene symbol", "bbox": {"x": 160.3378376087838, "y": 288.74999995089297, "width": 128.2702702702703, "height": 27.4999999999999}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Gene name", "bbox": {"x": 343.5810797640202, "y": 288.74999971354174, "width": 114.52702702702703, "height": 22.916666666666586}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Gene function", "bbox": {"x": 952.8648646358109, "y": 288.74999996527777, "width": 151.17567567567565, "height": 22.916666666666828}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Cell line", "bbox": {"x": 1255.1486383818342, "y": 288.5041635366093, "width": 82.59461449288044, "height": 23.40833832820255}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Array' Pos.'", "bbox": {"x": 1392.6486484195946, "y": 288.74999995416664, "width": 137.43243243243234, "height": 27.500000000000146}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "iMyc-1 iMyc-2", "bbox": {"x": 1218.5675673385135, "y": 348.3333332903645, "width": 155.75675675675686, "height": 27.500000000000146}}, {"kind": "text", "text": "Arc", "bbox": {"x": 160.33783732246624, "y": 412.49999971354174, "width": 41.22972972972973, "height":